    "stem": "A 44-year-old woman presents to a new primary care physician with a 3-year history of severe, disabling abdominal bloating, diffuse musculoskeletal aches, nausea, and frequent headaches. She has undergone extensive diagnostic evaluations over the past 3 years by multiple specialists, including four normal colonoscopies, two normal upper endoscopies, four abdominal and pelvic CT scans, three brain MRIs, and exhaustive rheumatologic panels, all of which have yielded completely normal results. Despite repeated reassurance from specialists, she spends 3 to 4 hours daily researching her symptoms online, constantly monitors her pulse and abdomen, and experiences overwhelming anxiety that physicians are missing a rare, fatal occult malignancy. Her symptoms have caused her to take extended medical leave from her job. Physical examination today demonstrates mild diffuse abdominal tenderness without guarding, rebound, or organomegaly, with a completely normal neurologic examination. Complete blood count, comprehensive metabolic panel, ESR, and thyroid testing are normal. Which of the following represents the most appropriate primary clinical management strategy?",
